{"id":"623b59f7-f85f-4861-9282-7924419f7954","object":"chat.completion","created":1784997610,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    items = case['items'][:]\\n    required = set(case['required_failure_items'])\\n    unresolved_without = set(case['unresolved_without'])\\n    test_count = 0\\n\\n    def test(subset):\\n        nonlocal test_count\\n        test_count += 1\\n        subset_set = set(subset)\\n        # UNRESOLVED if missing any unresolved_without item\\n        if unresolved_without and not unresolved_without.issubset(subset_set):\\n            return 'UNRESOLVED'\\n        if required.issubset(subset_set):\\n            return 'FAIL'\\n        return 'PASS'\\n\\n    # Initial configuration must be FAIL\\n    outcome = test(items)\\n    if outcome != 'FAIL':\\n        return {'minimal_failure': [], 'outcome': outcome, 'tests': test_count}\\n\\n    current = items[:]\\n    n = 2\\n    while len(current) > 1:\\n        # partition into n contiguous subsets\\n        chunk_size = (len(current) + n - 1) // n\\n        subsets = [current[i:i+chunk_size] for i in range(0, len(current), chunk_size)]\\n\\n        # test subsets first\\n        reduced = False\\n        for subset in subsets:\\n            if test(subset) == 'FAIL':\\n                current = subset[:]\\n                n = 2\\n                reduced = True\\n                break\\n        if reduced:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # test complements\\n        for subset in subsets:\\n            complement = [x for x in current if x not in subset]\\n            if test(complement) == 'FAIL':\\n                current = complement[:]\\n                n = max(2, n - 1)\\n                reduced = True\\n                break\\n        if reduced:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # no reduction: double granularity, cap at current size\\n        if n >= len(current):\\n            break\\n        n = min(n * 2, len(current))\\n\\n    final_outcome = test(current)\\n    return {\\n        'minimal_failure': current,\\n        'outcome': final_outcome,\\n        'tests': test_count\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement `solve(case)` for a delta debugging problem (ddmin). The payload has items, required_failure_items, unresolved_without. The goal: minimize a failure-inducing configuration. We have a deterministic oracle: given a subset of items, we can determine outcome (PASS, FAIL, UNRESOLVED). The oracle is not given explicitly, but we can infer from the case data? Actually, the case payload includes required_failure_items and unresolved_without. This seems to define the oracle: The full set (items) should produce FAIL? Let's read: \"Require the initial configuration to produce FAIL.\" So initial configuration is the full set of items. The oracle: a test on a subset returns:\n- FAIL if the subset contains all required_failure_items? Actually, typical ddmin: the test passes if the subset doesn't induce failure. But here we have \"failure-inducing\" meaning that certain items cause failure. The \"required_failure_items\" are items that must be present for failure? Or maybe they are the necessary items for failure? And \"unresolved_without\" are items that, if absent, make the outcome UNRESOLVED? The description: \"unresolved_without\" maybe items that, if not included, the test becomes unresolved. So we need to implement the oracle function. The typical delta debugging with PASS/FAIL/UNRESOLVED: given a configuration (subset of items), evaluate based on presence of required_failure_items and unresolved_without.\n\nInterpretation: The test fails (FAIL) if the subset includes all items in required_failure_items. It passes (PASS) if the subset does not include all required_failure_items and also doesn't trigger unresolved condition. It's unresolved (UNRESOLVED) if the subset does not include all items in unresolved_without? But unresolved_without maybe means items that if they are missing, the test is unresolved. More likely: unresolved_without list indicates items that must be present for the test to be resolved; if any of those are missing, outcome is UNRESOLVED. So we need to decide.\n\nLook at case examples:\nCase 0: items d0..d7, required_failure_items: [\"d1\",\"d4\"], unresolved_without: []. So full set contains d1,d4 -> FAIL. Any subset containing both d1 and d4 -> FAIL; otherwise PASS? Since no unresolved condition, all subsets are either FAIL (if contain both) or PASS (otherwise).\nCase 1: required_failure_items: [\"d0\",\"d7\"], unresolved_without: [] -> similar.\nCase 2: required_failure_items: [\"d4\",\"d7\"], unresolved_without: [\"d0\"]. So d0 must be present for the test to be resolved. If d0 missing -> UNRESOLVED. If d0 present and subset contains d4 and d7 -> FAIL. If d0 present but not both d4 and d7 -> PASS.\n\nThus oracle:\n- If any item in unresolved_without is not in subset -> UNRESOLVED.\n- Else if subset contains all required_failure_items -> FAIL.\n- Else -> PASS.\n\nThis matches typical \"unresolved\" semantics: missing a prerequisite leads to unknown outcome.\n\nNow we implement ddmin algorithm. The algorithm: start with full set. Test if it's FAIL (should be). Then recursively minimize: at current granularity, partition the current set into subsets, test subsets before complements. If a subset yields FAIL, reduce to that subset and restart from granularity 1? Actually classic ddmin: algorithm: ddmin(c) returns 1-minimal subset such that test(c) returns FAIL. Steps:\n- If |c| == 1: return c.\n- Let n = 2.\n- While True:\n    - Partition c into n subsets (roughly equal).\n    - For each subset in order:\n        - Test(subset). If FAIL: set c = subset, n = 2, break out of for loop and continue while (or restart? Actually after reduction, we go back to n=2? In classic ddmin, after a reduction we set n=2 and continue. But here we have \"decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size\". So after reduction, we set granularity to 2 (i.e., n=2). Otherwise, if no subset fails, we test complements. For each complement:\n            - Test(complement). If FAIL: set c = complement, n = max(2, n-1?) Actually \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" That suggests: If we reduce via subset, we decrease granularity (make n smaller, i.e., increase subset size? Actually granularity: number of subsets. Decreasing granularity means smaller n (fewer subsets, each larger). Doubling n otherwise. The algorithm: start with n=2. For each subset, test. If FAIL, set c=subset, set n=2 (decrease granularity to 2). If no subset fails, test complements. If a complement FAIL, set c=complement, and set n = max(2, n-1?) Actually \"double it up to current size\" means if no reduction, increase n (e.g., double) but not beyond size of c? Or \"up to current size\" might mean double n but cap at len(c). The wording: \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" That suggests: after a reduction (via subset or complement?), granularity decreases (n = previous n - 1? Or set to 2? But classic ddmin uses n=2 after reduction and doubles n after complement or no reduction? Actually classic: if subset fails, reduce to that subset and start again with n=2. If complement fails but no subset fails, reduce to complement and start again with n=2? Or keep n? Let's remember: The paper: ddmin algorithm: It starts with n=2. It tests each subset. If any subset is FAIL, set new c = that subset and set n=2, continue. If no subset but some complement FAIL, set new c = complement and set n = max(2, n-1). Otherwise, increase n = min(2*n, len(c)) and continue. That matches: after complement reduction, decrease granularity (n-1) but not below 2; after subset reduction, reset to 2. And if no reduction, double n.\n\nBut the instruction here: \"Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" The phrase \"after reduction\" could mean both subset and complement reductions? But \"double it up to current size\" suggests doubling if no reduction. And \"decrease granularity\" maybe means set n = max(2, n-1) as in classic? However, the spec: \"Partition at the current granularity and test subsets before complements. Only FAIL permits reduction; PASS and UNRESOLVED do not. Decrease granularity after reduction and otherwise double it up to current size.\" It doesn't distinguish subset vs complement. So I think after any reduction (subset or complement), we decrease granularity. But what does \"decrease granularity\" mean? Usually, granularity is the number of subsets; decreasing granularity makes subsets larger (fewer subsets). Could be n = max(2, n-1) or reset to 2. To be safe, let's adopt the classic algorithm: after subset reduction, reset to 2; after complement reduction, decrease by 1 (max 2). But we can verify with the provided cases? They are public fixtures, but we don't have expected outputs. We need to produce the correct results as per the fixture evaluator. Likely they expect the standard ddmin algorithm from the literature (Zeller's delta debugging). Since the task is \"ddmin_with_unresolved\", they likely expect the algorithm that handles UNRESOLVED.\n\nGiven the public fixture payloads, let's simulate manually to see what results might be.\n\nCase 0: items 0..7, required: d1,d4, no unresolved.\nFull set -> FAIL.\nStart n=2. Partition into two subsets: {d0,d1,d2,d3} and {d4,d5,d6,d7}. Test each:\nSubset1: contains d1 but not d4 -> PASS (since not both required). Subset2: contains d4 but not d1 -> PASS. No subset FAIL.\nNow test complements: complement of subset1 is subset2 -> same as before? Actually complement of subset1 is subset2, which is PASS. Complement of subset2 is subset1, PASS. So no complement FAIL. So no reduction. Double n->4.\nNow partition current c (full set) into 4 subsets: {d0,d1}, {d2