{d4,d5}: missing d0 -> UNRESOLVED.\n{d6,d7}: missing d0 -> UNRESOLVED.\nNo subset FAIL (since none contain both d4,d7 and d0). \nComplements:\nComplement of {d0,d1} = {d2,d3,d4,d5,d6,d7} -> missing d0 -> UNRESOLVED.\nComplement of {d2,d3} = {d0,d1,d4,d5,d6,d7} -> contains d0, and d4,d7 -> FAIL! So reduce c to that complement, n = max(2,3)=3. c = {d0,d1,d4,d5,d6,d7} size6.\nn=3. Partition into 3 subsets: {d0,d1}, {d4,d5}, {d6,d7}.\nTest:\n{d0,d1}: has d0, no d4,d7 -> PASS.\n{d4,d5}: missing d0 -> UNRESOLVED.\n{d6,d7}: missing d0 -> UNRESOLVED.\nNo subset FAIL.\nComplements:\nComplement of {d0,d1} = {d4,d5,d6,d7} -> missing d0 -> UNRESOLVED.\nComplement of {d4,d5} = {d0,d1,d6,d7} -> has d0, contains d7 but not d4 -> PASS? Actually contains d7 but not d4 -> PASS (since need both d4 and d7 for FAIL). So PASS.\nComplement of {d6,d7} = {d0,d1,d4,d5} -> has d0, contains d4 but not d7 -> PASS.\nNo complement FAIL. Double n = min(6, 2*3)=6. n=6.\nPartition c into 6 subsets: {d0}, {d1}, {d4}, {d5}, {d6}, {d7} (size1 each).\nTest each:\n{d0}: has d0, no d4,d7 -> PASS.\n{d1}: missing d0 -> UNRESOLVED.\n{d4}: missing d0 -> UNRESOLVED.\n{d5}: missing d0 -> UNRESOLVED.\n{d6}: missing d0 -> UNRESOLVED.\n{d7}: missing d0 -> UNRESOLVED.\nNo subset FAIL.\nComplements:\nComplement of {d0} = {d1,d4,d5,d6,d7} -> missing d0 -> UNRESOLVED.\nComplement of {d1} = {d0,d4,d5,d6,d7} -> has d0, contains d4,d7 -> FAIL! So reduce c to {d0,d4,d5,d6,d7}. n = max(2,5)=5? (n was 6, n-1=5). c size=5.\nn=5. Partition into 5 subsets: size1 each: {d0},{d4},{d5},{d6},{d7}.\nTest:\n{d0}: PASS; {d4}: missing d0 -> UNRESOLVED; {d5}: UNRESOLVED; {d6}: UNRESOLVED; {d7}: UNRESOLVED.\nNo subset FAIL.\nComplements:\nComplement of {d0} = {d4,d5,d6,d7} -> missing d0 -> UNRESOLVED.\nComplement of {d4} = {d0,d5,d6,d7} -> has d0, contains d7 but not d4 -> PASS.\nComplement of {d5} = {d0,d4,d6,d7} -> has d0, contains d4 and d7 -> FAIL! Reduce to {d0,d4,d6,d7}. n = max(2,4)=4. c size=4.\nn=4. Partition into 4 singletons: {d0},{d4},{d6},{d7}.\nTest:\n{d0}: PASS; {d4}: UNRESOLVED; {d6}: UNRESOLVED; {d7}: UNRESOLVED.\nComplements:\nComplement of {d0} = {d4,d6,d7} -> missing d0 -> UNRESOLVED.\nComplement of {d4} = {d0,d6,d7} -> has d0, contains d7 but not d4 -> PASS.\nComplement of {d6} = {d0,d4,d7} -> has d0, contains d4 and d7 -> FAIL! Reduce to {d0,d4,d7}. n = max(2,3)=3. c size=3.\nn=3. Partition into 3 singletons: {d0},{d4},{d7}.\nTest:\n{d0}: PASS; {d4}: UNRESOLVED; {d7}: UNRESOLVED.\nComplements:\nComplement of {d0} = {d4,d7} -> missing d0 -> UNRESOLVED.\nComplement of {d4} = {d0,d7} -> has d0, contains d7 but not d4 -> PASS.\nComplement of {d7} = {d0,d4} -> has d0, contains d4 but not d7 -> PASS.\nNo reduction. Since n == len(c)=3, we return c = {d0,d4,d7}. That is the 1-minimal failure. Minimal set that includes d0, d4, d7. Removing any of the three would cause either UNRESOLVED or PASS. So minimal_failure = [\"d0\",\"d4\",\"d7\"]. outcome = \"FAIL\". tests = count.\n\nThus algorithm works. Now need to implement in Python. We'll define `solve(case)` that takes the payload dict with keys 'items', 'required_failure_items', 'unresolved_without'. We'll implement the oracle function inside. We'll implement ddmin algorithm with test counting. Return dict with 'minimal_failure' (list), 'outcome' (string: \"FAIL\" if final set fails, else \"PASS\" or \"UNRESOLVED\"? But algorithm ensures final set fails because we only continue if we have a failing set. Initially must be FAIL. So outcome should be \"FAIL\". But if somehow initial not FAIL, but spec requires it. We'll compute outcome from oracle on final set.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Use a list for current set (ordered as in original items? We'll treat items as list, and subsets as lists preserving relative order? For partitioning, we need to maintain order? Delta debugging typically works on ordered configurations. The partition should preserve the original order. We'll keep current set as a list (ordered). When partitioning into n subsets, we split the list into contiguous chunks of roughly equal size. For complements, we compute the complement of a subset within current set.\n- Test function: given subset (list), return \"PASS\", \"FAIL\", or \"UNRESOLVED\" based on conditions:\n    - if any item in unresolved_without not in subset -> UNRESOLVED\n    - elif all items in required_failure_items are in subset -> FAIL\n    - else -> PASS\n- Ensure we don't mutate original case; we copy items list.\n- Count tests: increment each time we call test.\n- Use loops: while len(c) > 1? Actually algorithm terminates when no further reduction possible; we need to implement as described.\n\nPseudo:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    items = case['items'][:]  # list\n    required = set(case['required_failure_items'])\n    unresolved_without = set(case['unresolved_without'])\n    test_count = 0\n    def test(subset):\n        nonlocal test_count\n        test_count += 1\n        subset_set = set(subset)\n        # check unresolved\n        if unresolved_without and not unresolved_without.issubset(subset_set):\n            return \"UNRESOLVED\"\n        if required.issubset(subset_set):\n            return \"FAIL\"\n        return \"PASS\"\n    \n    # initial test on full set; should be FAIL\n    outcome = test(items)\n    if outcome != \"FAIL\":\n        # but spec says require initial FAIL; if not, return empty? but we'll handle gracefully\n        return {\"minimal_failure\": [], \"outcome\": outcome, \"tests\": test_count}\n    \n    current = items[:]\n    n = 2\n    while len(current) > 1:\n        # partition current into n subsets\n        subsets = []\n        chunk_size = (len(current) + n - 1) // n\n        for i in range(0, len(current), chunk_size):\n            subsets.append(current[i:i+chunk_size])\n        # test subsets\n        reduced = False\n        for subset in subsets:\n            res = test(subset)\n            if res == \"FAIL\":\n                current = subset[:]\n                n = 2\n                reduced = True\n                break\n        if reduced:\n            continue\n        # test complements\n        for subset in subsets:\n            # complement = current without subset elements, preserving order\n            complement = [x for x in current if x not in subset]\n            res = test(complement)\n            if res == \"FAIL\":\n                current = complement[:]\n                n = max(2, n - 1)\n                reduced = True\n                break\n        if reduced:\n            continue\n        # no reduction\n        if n >= len(current):\n            break\n        n = min(n * 2, len(current))\n    \n    # final outcome\n    final_outcome = test(current)\n    return {\n        \"minimal_failure\": current,\n        \"outcome\": final_outcome,\n        \"tests\": test_count\n    }\n\nBut note: In the while loop, we break if n >= len(current) and no reduction. Also we need to handle case where len(current) reduces to 1; then we return. Also we need to ensure test_count counts all tests including final one. In the loop we also test subsets and complements, so count is incremented.\n\nEdge case: unresolved_without may be empty; then condition unresolved_without.issubset(subset_set) is vacuously true.\n\nOrder of minimal_failure: should we preserve original order? The list 'current' will preserve the order from the original items because we always take slices or list comprehensions preserving relative order. That seems fine.\n\nNow we need to produce the output as JSON string within the \"implementation\" key. The response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value of the Python source code as an escaped JSON string. So we need to write the Python code and then serialize it into a JSON string, escaping properly. Since the response format is strict, we must output raw JSON, no markdown.\n\nI'll write the Python function with proper indentation and comments. Then encode it as a JSON string